<article class="env-proposition" id="DEC8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/DEC8">Proposition <span data-tag="DEC8">6.1.2.11</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category, and let $U_{\bullet }^{+}: \Nerve (\cDelta _{+})^{op} \rightarrow \calC $ be an augmented simplicial object of $\calC $. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The augmented simplicial object $U_{\bullet }^{+}$ is a right Kan extension of $U_{\bullet }^{+}| \Nerve (\cDelta ^{\leq 0}_{+})^{op}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The underlying simplicial object $U_{\bullet }$ is a groupoid object of $\calC $, and the diagram $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \Nerve ( \cDelta ^{\leq 1}_{+})^{op}$ is a pullback square 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_{1} \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_0 \ar [d] \\ U_{0} \ar [r] &  U_{-1} } \]
</div>
<p>
 in the $\infty $-category $\calC $. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose first that $(1)$ is satisfied. It follows immediately from the definition of right Kan extensions that the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_{1} \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_0 \ar [d] \\ U_{0} \ar [r] &  U_{-1} } \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a pullback. To prove that $U_{\bullet }$ is a groupoid, we show that $U_{\bullet }$ satisfies criterion $(4'')$ of Proposition <a href="/tag/D95F" data-tag="D95F">6.1.2.6</a>. Let $S$ and $S'$ be sets with union $[n]$ and intersection $S \cap S' = \{ s\} $. Let $\calI $ be the nerve of the category $(\cDelta _{+})_{/\Delta ^ n}$. For each subset $J \subseteq [n]$, let $\calI (J)$ denote the full subcategory of $\calI $ spanned by the initial object together with the inclusions $\{ j\}  \rightarrow \Delta ^ n$, $j \in S$. By assumption, $U_{\bullet }^{+}$ exhibits $U_{\bullet }(S)$ as a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \Nerve (\calI (S))$, $U_{\bullet }(S')$ as a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \Nerve (\calI (S'))$, $U_{\bullet }([n])$ as a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \Nerve (\calI ( [n] ))$, and $U_{\bullet }( \{ s\}  )$ as a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \Nerve (\calI (\{ s\} ))$. It follows from Corollary <a href="/tag/7BD9" data-tag="7BD9">4.2.3.10</a> that the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_{\bullet }( [n] ) \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_{\bullet }(S) \ar [d] \\ U_{\bullet }(S') \ar [r] &  U_{\bullet }( \{ s\}  ) } \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a pullback. 
    <p>
      We now prove that $(2)$ implies $(1)$. Using the above notation, we must show that for each $n \geq -1$, $U_{\bullet }^{+}$ exhibits $U_{\bullet }^{+}([n])$ as a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \calI ( [n] )$. For $n \leq 0$, this is obvious; for $n=1$ it is equivalent to the assumption that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_{1} \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_0 \ar [d] \\ U_{0} \ar [r] &  U_{-1} } \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a pullback diagram. We prove the general case by induction on $n$. Using the inductive hypothesis, we conclude that $U_{\bullet }(\Delta ^{S})$ is a limit of $U_{\bullet }^{+} | \calI (S)$ for all <em>proper</em> subsets $S \subset [n]$. Choose a decomposition $\{ 0, \ldots , n\}  = S \cup S'$, where $S \cap S' = \{ s\} $. According to Proposition <a href="/tag/7644" data-tag="7644">4.4.2.2</a>, the desired result is equivalent to the assertion that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { U_{\bullet }([n]) \ar [r] \ar [d] &  U_{\bullet }(S) \ar [d] \\ U_{\bullet }(S') \ar [r] &  U_{\bullet }( \{ s\}  ) } \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a pullback diagram, which follows from our assumption that $U_{\bullet }$ is a groupoid object of $\calC $. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>